{-
   A class function may not keep what it was lent.

   The rule a *deferred* dispatch takes on trust - see `assumedRetained` in analyze_escape.cpp. A
   call site that selected the instance reads the implementation's own summary and is told what it
   kept; a call site inside a generic body names the class signature, which has no body, so what it
   believes instead is the declaration: a borrow's extent is the call. This is where that belief is
   paid for, one implementation at a time, so that a generic body handing `update` a view of its own
   buffer is not taking the word of whichever instance a caller happens to select.

   `->` is the answer to every diagnostic below, and it is what `IndexInsert.insertAt` already writes:
   an insert that misses *stores* the key, so it asks for the key rather than borrowing it.

   This is the ownership stage's own rule. Two lines are
   respelled for the current language rather than for the rule: a loan field is re-pointed by an
   update of its record, since assigning to one writes through it (ownership.md, "Records holding
   external values"), and `Box` holds the window itself rather than a `Holder` that does - a record
   nesting a loan-holding record is refused at the declaration in this stage, which would have
   been the only thing `keepInside` reported.

   The messages were not the shipping stage's "this implements a class function" and that was not a
   gap. This stage holds *every* body to what its own signature says a loan's extent is - a global
   to the program's, a `&` parameter's field to a group the caller binds - so an
   implementation whose signature borrows `xs` for the call cannot keep it anywhere, class member or
   not, and a generic body that trusts the class declaration is trusting a rule that was checked.
-}
data Holder {view: '[Int], at: Int}

-- Over a global's run rather than a `[0]` literal, whose window would be of a temporary. That is
-- accepted today only because a borrow of a value with no storage carries no loan.
let &sinkRun = [0] :: Array(Int)
let &sink = Holder {view: sinkRun, at: 0}

pub class Keep(c):
  fn keepGlobally(&self: c, xs: '[Int]) -> {}
  fn keepInside(&self: c, xs: '[Int]) -> {}
  fn readOnly(&self: c, xs: '[Int]) -> Int

data Box {n: Int, view: '[Int]}

instance Keep(Box):
    -- Into a global, which outlives every frame there is.
    fn keepGlobally(&self: Box, xs: '[Int]):
        sink = {sink | view: xs}

    -- And into storage the caller passed in, which outlives this frame for the same reason.
    fn keepInside(&self: Box, xs: '[Int]):
        self = {self | view: xs}

    -- The control: reading through the borrow is what a borrow is for, and is not reported.
    fn readOnly(&self: Box, xs: '[Int]) -> Int = xs[0] + self.n

fn main() -> Int = 0
